
>> SLA/SLS

!

>  Resin (regular/water-soluble)
! remember to avoid breathing the fumes and directly touching the resin 

* before pouring in resin, stir/shake your filament bottle to make sure the different elements within it are mixed properly
* post-print resin isn't as clear and effective, so reusing it for more prints is problematic
* You should filter used dissolving solution (water/IPA) for re-use.
* * Put it in the sun or UV-light (best if solution is in a glass container), then filter it. using a coffee filter or cheese-cloth (multi-use)
* * * You can filter using:
* * * * a metal sieve + funnel
* * * * a coffee filter in a double-funnel (specific model by VogMan - inner funnel is perforated so there's more surface area for the coffee filter to filter through)
* * * * cheese cloth (multi-use, and can be wrung dry, with gloves of-course)
* * * Throw the solid plastic remains in the trash
* * maybe: consider immediately reusing the resin within your tray after print - it's not as clean, but you can get some more use out of it before losing some to the filtering process...
* Wash FEB with warm water and dish-soap
* * Personal experience: dish-soap is good at cleaning resin (non water-soluble) from skin

> Water-soluble Resin
* Still toxic! Don't clean your parts by just washing them in the sink, and don't drain it uncured either!
* After washing with water, it's still soft enough to easily remove supports
* * Warm water makes it easier to remove support
- - Prints are slimy after their wash, and require wiping
- - - The less-effective cleaning is even more problematic for hollow prints, as inner unreached resin oozes out over time (same problem as regular resin, but amplified)
+ Cleaning prints is cheaper (tap water is much cheaper than IPA)
+ Water isn't flammable like IPA
+ Water doesn't cause odors like IPA (actually harmful because of the mixed-in resin?)
- Prints are more brittle/softer compared to "regular" resin, so it's not suitable for thin parts (miniatures with swords/spears)
- If you cure prints that still have water on them, you get white marks on your prints
+ + There are "tough" variants (supposedly)

> Recommended resins
* Recommendations from https://www.youtube.com/@GrayScalpMiniatures
* * Elegoo ABS-like
* * ChituSystems Conjure

> Washing
* Get a pickle-jar (liftable plastic basket within a jar) for rough cleaning, pre-ultrasound-cleaning phase ; details here:  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oCgDm1KfboY
* * Get 2 for thorough cleaning
* You can use warm water (even on regular resin prints) to heat up your print so the supports separate better
* For non water-washable resin:
* * you can use Methylated Spirit instead of IPA 
+ + + Odorless
+ + + Doesn't accumulate sludge, so you can keep using the same solution without replacement
* * 50% alcohol is about as good as over 90% if you wash thoroughly enough (you'd lose quality over time on reuse anyway)
* For water-solution
* * pure water (no minerals/salts) is better than plain tap-water



